-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls**
-*   **Texture Filtering:** The visual quality (block-style vs. smooth) depends on the 3D texture filtering mode (nearest vs. linear) set on the GPU [Parent].
-*   **Back-to-Front Order:** Correct alpha compositing requires the geometry to be rendered in back-to-front order relative to the viewer [S1].
+**Parameters:**
+*   `volume`: Type `vzParameterVolumeTexture`. Specifies the actual volume data to be rendered [S1]. This is the only required parameter for `vzTMSimpleShader` [S1].
+
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Blending Order:** The geometry must be rendered in back-to-front order to ensure correct alpha blending [S1]. The `vzTMRenderAction` handles this sorting [S1].
+*   **Texture Binding:** The `"volume"` texture must be bound during the rendering pass [S1].
+*   **Data Type:** The volume data must be compatible with the `vzParameterVolumeTexture` type [S1, S2].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### Tagging Shader
-A variant that performs volumetric tagging using two perfectly overlapping volumes: a data volume and a tag volume acting as a 3D stencil buffer [S1]. If the value of the tag texel is greater than 0.5, the corresponding texel in the volume data is rendered; otherwise, it is masked out [S1]. This method avoids modifying the original volume data but incurs the overhead of storing an additional 3D texture [S1]. On non-GPU systems or those lacking fragment shader extensions (ARB_fragment_program/ATI_fragment_shader), it uses the OpenGL stencil buffer, requiring the application to ensure the appropriate visual is selected [S1].
-
 ### vzTMLUTShader
-A variant that applies transfer functions to volume data using a 1D lookup table (LUT) mapped to interpolated texel values [S1]. It requires two parameters: `volume` and `lookup_table` [S1]. Unlike pre-computing colors for each texel, which requires huge computation and re-downloading the whole volume on transfer function changes, this shader applies the transfer function via OpenGL hardware, requiring only the LUT to be downloaded on changes [S1]. The implementation is system-dependent [S1].
+A shader that applies transfer functions to volume data using a 1D lookup table (LUT) mapped to interpolated texel values [S1]. It solves the problem of dynamic color mapping without the overhead of pre-computing colors for each texel [S1]. Unlike `vzTMSimpleShader`, which renders raw values, `vzTMLUTShader` maps scalar values to colors via a LUT [S1]. It requires two parameters: `volume` (`vzParameterVolumeTexture`) and `lookup_table` (`vzParameterLookupTable`) [S1]. A key advantage is that when the transfer function changes, only the LUT needs to be downloaded to texture memory, which is faster than re-downloading the entire volume texture [S1]. However, the process is system-dependent in its implementation via OpenGL hardware [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Complexity:** Time complexity is linear with respect to the number of fragments/pixels covered by the volume geometry. Memory complexity is dominated by the storage of the 3D volume texture in GPU memory [S1].
-*   **Performance:** This is the fastest rendering method among the built-in shaders because it offloads all computation to the texture hardware without requiring per-fragment gradient calculations or complex shading logic [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** It does not support lighting, shadows, or surface normal estimation, resulting in a flat appearance unless the volume data itself encodes such information [S1]. It is suitable for quick visualization or when the volume data is already pre-shaded [S1].
-*   **Hardware Dependency:** The quality and performance depend on the GPU's 3D texture support and filtering capabilities [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources. The rendering time depends on the resolution of the generated polygonal geometry and the cost of back-to-front sorting [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the `vzParameterVolumeTexture` in GPU memory [S1, S2].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources. A 256 MB CT of a human head is rendered using this shader [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation; leverages hardware texture interpolation and blending; no custom shading logic required [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** No transfer function application; limited visual control compared to LUT or gradient shaders [S1].
+*   **When to use:** When a quick visualization of raw volume data is needed without color mapping [S1].
+*   **When not to use:** When specific tissue types or data ranges need to be highlighted via color mapping (use `vzTMLUTShader`) or when masking specific regions is required (use `vzTMTagShader`) [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The Simple Shader is part of the SGI OpenGL Volumizer library, specifically the Texture Mapping Render Action (TMRenderAction) [S1]. It was introduced as a basic volume rendering technique that leverages standard 3D texture mapping capabilities available in OpenGL hardware [S1, S2]. It is often used as a baseline for comparing more advanced shading techniques like gradient-based shading or tangent-space shading [S1].
+The Simple Shader is part of the SGI VolumePro or similar volumetric rendering toolkit, specifically within the `TMRenderAction` framework [S1]. It was documented in Chapter 4 of the SGI HTML manual "Texture Mapping Render Action" [S1]. The approach builds on the general technique of 3D texture mapping for volume rendering, where the volume is sliced into polygons and rendered with 3D textures [S1]. The `vzTMSimpleShader` class is a built-in shader provided by the toolkit [S1].
 
